{"benchmark": "livebench_selected", "task_id": "paraphrase:f3472a74bc480a915c2a3875c505fd5b3ebe475d25cefdae9447002ed88b0106", "sample_idx": 0, "model_name": "mistralai/mistral-small-4-119b-2603", "content": "Oregon’s pioneering attempt to decriminalize drugs will conclude this weekend, as a fresh statute reintroduces penalties for possessing small quantities of narcotics. Starting Sunday, a revised measure takes effect, making it illegal again to carry substances like fentanyl, heroin, and meth. Those caught face either prosecution or the option of enrollment in treatment programs, which include finishing mental health courses and joining intervention initiatives to steer clear of fines. Simple possession will now be treated as a minor offense, carrying potential jail time of up to half a year. The updated law also strengthens enforcement against public drug use and imposes stricter consequences for trafficking near sensitive locations such as schools or parks.\n\nThe legislation promotes, though it does not enforce, the creation of local treatment alternatives to guide individuals away from legal consequences and toward assistance for addiction and psychological issues. Advocates argue this flexibility lets regions tailor programs to their available means, but critics warn it could lead to inconsistent and unfair enforcement across different locales. “The rules for avoiding prosecution are quite restrictive: no prior offenses, no outstanding warrants, no aggressive conduct, and stable health,” explained Portland’s police commissioner.\n\nIf someone qualifies for diversion, officers must contact the designated county team within thirty minutes to assess eligibility and link them to immediate aid. “If behavioral health workers cannot arrive promptly, the individual will be taken into custody,” the chief stated. “We cannot delay indefinitely.”\n\nThe shift marks the end of Oregon’s groundbreaking but short-lived drug decriminalization effort, replacing it with a system that balances penalties with optional support pathways.\n\n***\n\nP.P.S Oregon’s move reflects a broader ['age']-related debate on drug policy, as the ['country'] grapples with addiction challenges. Some residents now seek alternative ways to ['eat'] local ['shopping'] needs, while debates over ['tongue']-in-cheek slogans like “just say no” resurface.", "logprobs": [{"token": "O", "logprob": -0.016314569860696793}, {"token": "regon", "logprob": -3.3378546504536644e-06}, {"token": "’s", "logprob": -0.12892691791057587}, {"token": " pioneering", "logprob": -0.8294546604156494}, {"token": " attempt", "logprob": -2.0091590881347656}, {"token": " to", "logprob": -0.0790967270731926}, {"token": " dec", "logprob": -0.34582358598709106}, {"token": "riminal", "logprob": -3.933898824470816e-06}, {"token": "ize", "logprob": -1.156323378381785e-05}, {"token": " drugs", "logprob": -0.4133265018463135}, {"token": " will", "logprob": -0.4553186297416687}, {"token": " conclude", "logprob": -0.47528043389320374}, {"token": " this", "logprob": -1.732130765914917}, {"token": " weekend", "logprob": -0.0759149119257927}, {"token": ",", "logprob": -0.8811122179031372}, {"token": " as", "logprob": -0.816861629486084}, {"token": " a", "logprob": -0.01860828325152397}, {"token": " fresh", "logprob": -1.2513847351074219}, {"token": " statute", "logprob": -1.7214211225509644}, {"token": " reintrodu", "logprob": -1.095862627029419}, {"token": "ces", "logprob": -0.0141721460968256}, {"token": " penalties", "logprob": -0.01646408997476101}, {"token": " for", "logprob": -3.6954811548639555e-06}, {"token": " possessing", "logprob": -0.7775425910949707}, {"token": " small", "logprob": -0.06078707054257393}, {"token": " quantities", "logprob": -0.10035669058561325}, {"token": " of", "logprob": -0.00285206432454288}, {"token": " narc", "logprob": -2.15464448928833}, {"token": "otics", "logprob": -3.3378546504536644e-06}, {"token": ".", "logprob": -0.0041547417640686035}, {"token": " Starting", "log